To start with the conclusion, I personally think that “Four Seas” is a complete mess, in every sense of the word.
But if you're going to say that this movie is not a highlight, then at least the music is pretty good.
There are many kinds of bad movies, and one kind is that which can make you fall asleep in the cinema, while another is that which can make you feel indignant after watching it. For the audience, “Four Seas” is like a monkey on Mount Emei, and it can ignite your anger in countless ways.
In terms of insulting the audience, Han Han is truly a gifted person.
The most painful thing about this film is that it seems to be a creature in the novel of the Ke family, indescribable, and full of holes like the trench coat on Xiao Mago.
Even after you've seen it, you wonder if there's some kind of metaphor in there that the director is trying to hide, but when you think about it, it's just a pile of shit.
Let me start with a few things that I find hard to understand.
The male protagonist lives on a small island in the south and is dependent on his grandmother.
Her grandmother had a wish to see snow, so the plot arranged for a neighbor to take her to the northeast to see snow in a small van.
An elderly person takes a minibus from the south to the northeast to see the snow?
He and his father are very confident about this.
And then the old man was never heard from again.
How did the female protagonist's brother get a business license to open a hotel on an abandoned ship?
Why can the small gang of motorcyclists on the island easily block a mountain road?
Then the truck in the middle of the road carrying the steel bars stopped on the roadside, and the truck driver was nowhere to be found.
This prop sense is too obvious.
Why would a small gangster who has a bad credit record and is suspected of breaking the law become an auxiliary police officer?
Why didn't you call the police when facing high-interest loans? The police in the movie specially asked whether there was any difficulty.
An adult who knows how to use a smartphone but does not know how to open the door of a hotel with a room card, and then forcibly arrange to find the hotel owner, sit outside the door of the room for a night?
This is a recurring theme throughout the film, and one that I find painful to recall, as the plot is so far-fetched.
Beyond the plot, the film's message is worse, or rather the film has no message at all, just a repulsive Frankenstein's monster.
At the beginning of the film, you will think he wants to make a comedy. After more than 20 minutes, you will guess that it may be an inspirational film. After watching half of the film, you will think that it may be a film that reflects the difficult survival of young people in small towns. After watching the film, you will not know what you have watched. This is followed by deep self-doubt. Why should you go to the cinema to watch feces during the Spring Festival?
For most people, they are very familiar with the routines of various movies. For example, if you want to shoot a motorcycle rider, then it is inspirational + comedy and then tell the audience how a talented novice moves towards success step by step.
If you want to make a love story, then you should just tell the story of the love and hate between a small town youth who likes to ride motorcycles and a waitress.
If you want to reflect reality, then at least your plot should not have things that are contrary to the normal world.
(For example, the new motorcycle that was just burned and then could sing), it is impossible to go some brain holes that even Kennedy can't stand.
As for the female lead, who is clearly not a weakling but behaves like a 5-year-old in some aspects, I really don't know whether it's due to acting skills or the director's requirements.
I also don't understand why Han Han, as a race car driver, is so keen on adding a car crash scene in his own movie.
Is such a film arranged in the Spring Festival to add trouble to the audience?
