South African doctor: Omicron is highly contagious, but most are mild cases
Many doctors and medical experts in South Africa believe that the symptoms caused by the Omicron variant of coronavirus are milder than those caused by the Delta variant, although it spreads faster.
According to the Associated Press, as the Omicron variant spreads in South Africa, a medical organization composed of more than 5,000 doctors in Gauteng Province reported that the symptoms of the COVID-19 patients they currently treat are relatively mild, such as body pain and cough, which are similar to flu symptoms.
Dr Unben Pillay, the organisation's director, said he sees dozens of patients every day but none of them have been hospitalised.
Pillay said, "They can control the disease at home, and most people recover within 10 to 14 days of isolation. "
He said this includes elderly patients and those with underlying health conditions, who are more likely to become seriously ill from the coronavirus infection.
He said that in comparison, during the last wave of Delta infections, he treated COVID-19 patients who "were struggling to breathe and had low oxygen levels.
Many people will be hospitalized within a few days. "
Within two weeks of the first report of the Omicron variant in southern Africa, other doctors shared similar experiences.
Although it will take several weeks to collect enough data, doctors' observations and early evidence provide some clues.
According to the summary of the National Institute for Communicable Diseases of South Africa: among the COVID-19 patients who were hospitalized in recent weeks, only 30% were serious cases, which was half of the proportion of serious cases in the previous wave; the average hospital stay was shortened from 8 days to 2.8 days; among the recent COVID-19 patients, only 3% died, while the death rate was 20% in the early outbreak cases in South Africa.
According to data from the National Institute for Infectious Diseases and other reports, Willem Hanekom, director of the African Health Research Institute, said, "At present, almost all signs indicate that this is a mild disease. "
He also said, "It is still early and we need to get later data.
Usually, hospitalization and death occur later, and we have been in this wave of the epidemic for only two weeks. "
Meanwhile, scientists around the world are watching case numbers and hospitalization rates while testing the effectiveness of current vaccines and treatments.
Although Delta remains the dominant strain of the virus worldwide, Omicron cases are emerging in dozens of countries, with South Africa at the epicenter of the outbreak.
Gauteng has seen a 400% increase in new cases in the first week of December, with more than 90% of cases now testing positive for the Omicron variant, health officials said.
