The International Olive Oil Council classifies olive oil into two major categories, virgin olive oil and lampante olive oil or refined olive oil, with five levels.
Virgin olive oil, also known as natural olive oil, is the oil obtained directly from fresh olives by mechanical cold pressing and then filtered to remove impurities.
They are divided into three levels according to their acidity:
Extra Virgin Olive Oil: It is the highest grade and highest quality olive oil, which is a natural product.
It has a great taste with a delicate and pleasant plant fragrance, and an acid content of less than 1%.
Fine Virgin: Slightly higher acidity, but not more than 2%, pure and fragrant.
Ordinary Virgin: The taste and flavor are still acceptable, with an acid content not exceeding 3.3%.
Refined olive oil is the olive oil obtained from the refining of virgin olive oil with an acid content above 3.3%, or "second oil".
Refined olive oil is divided into two grades:
Olive Oil: Refined olive oil mixed with a certain proportion of extra virgin olive oil to blend the taste and color, with acidity below 1.5%, is transparent light golden yellow.
Refined olive-pomace oil: is extracted from the pomace by dissolution and refined to obtain olive oil.
You should have noticed by now that the acidity of olive oil is a very important indicator, as per the classification.
It should be noted that olives are like grapes used to make wine, and different standards of olive oil will be produced from different regions and different varieties of olives.
The best place to grow olive oil:
The Mediterranean coast has the most suitable soil and climate conditions such as temperature, sunshine, air humidity and precipitation required for the growth of oil olives.
Except for the Mediterranean coast, other areas can only be sporadically planted in suitable areas.
Main production areas (Mediterranean):
Italy, Spain, Greece, Turkey, Portugal, Morocco, Syria, Israel, Tunisia, Algeria, Egypt, Pakistan, Lebanon, and so on, and those with similar climate in the far south latitude, such as Argentina, Peru, South Africa and so on.
Olive harvesting month:
From March to June every year, it is the time for the olive tree to bloom and bear fruit. From July to October, the fruits begin to develop and mature, and the fruits transition from green to red, purple, and finally black.
The fruit is picked from November to February of the next year (the freshness of olive oil is very important).
Nutritional composition of olive oil:
Olive oil is rich in monounsaturated fatty acids, i.e., oleic acid and linoleic acid, linolenic acid, as well as vitamin A, vitamin B, vitamin D, vitamin E, vitamin K and antioxidants.
Olive oil is considered to be the most suitable oil for human nutrition among all oils discovered so far.
Biden says 'fire of freedom' will never be extinguished as democracy summit ends
The online "Summit for Democracy" convened by US President Biden and attended by government leaders, civil society and private sector leaders from 110 countries around the world ended on Friday (December 10).
The torch of freedom burns in everyone's heart around the world, and authoritarian regimes can never extinguish it, Biden said in his closing remarks.
He also said that he planned to hold the second global "Democracy Summit" next year.
Tang Fenghe, a representative of the Taiwan government and a member of the Executive Yuan, and Luo Guansheng, a Hong Kong pro-democracy and human rights activist currently in exile in the UK, also spoke at the summit on the second day.
Biden said, "We know how difficult the work ahead of us is.
But we also know that we can meet the challenge, because as I have said before, and as this summit has shown, the democratic world is everywhere.
The dictatorship can never extinguish the small fire of freedom, which burns in the world and in the hearts of people all over the world. "
Biden also said that defending democracy requires the joint efforts of the whole society.
He said, "As leaders of the government, we have a responsibility to listen to our citizens, strengthen the guardrails of democracy, promote reform, achieve transparent accountability governance, and be more resistant to the buffer and impact of autocracy, as well as those who want to pursue power nakedly before the public interest. "
The themes of the second day of the "Democracy Summit" are defending human rights, consolidating democracy and confronting authoritarianism, as well as countering digital authoritarianism and affirming democratic values.
This day coincides with "International Human Rights Day".
In his opening remarks, UN Secretary-General Guterres also said that the cause of democracy faces challenges globally, especially those brought about by emerging digital technologies.
He said, "It is time to reaffirm common values and positions as well as democratic resilience.
This requires deepening social dialogue, addressing inequality, fighting corruption, renewing the social contract, investing in quality education and expanding opportunities for women and girls.
This also requires a firm condemnation of all forms of populism, nativism and extremism, including those that exploit all the tools provided by technological revolution. "
Hong Kong pro-democracy and human rights activist Luo Guansheng addressed the summit through a pre-recorded video, reminding participants to continue to pay attention to the human rights situation in Hong Kong and ethnic minorities in China such as Uyghurs and Tibetans.
He said, "For a long time, the world has been accepting China's rise, but has not established a mechanism to hold it accountable.
This is why politicians and civil society leaders must work together.
We must use everything we have (resources) to ensure that the revival of democracy is our top priority. "
Lo also called on the free world to unite against the threat of the CCP.
He said, "Maybe some of you are afraid to upset General Secretary Xi Jinping, maybe some of you don't want to lose the Chinese market, maybe some of you don't realize the threat (of the CCP) to our democratic values.
And that's why we failed.
This is also the reason why we must change. "
Tang Feng, a member of the Executive Yuan of Taiwan, delivered a video-recorded “National Statement” at the round-table discussion on “Combating Digital Authoritarianism and Upholding Democratic Values”, stressing that Taiwan is at the forefront of the fight against dictatorship and authoritarianism.
He also shared his experience on how to fight the COVID-19 epidemic through information transparency and building mutual trust.
He said, "At a time when Taiwan's democracy is still in its infancy, we have always stood firmly at the forefront of the global fight against authoritarianism, and we have played a leading role in promoting global freedom, democracy, and human rights.
During the pandemic, authoritarian governments around the world have rationalized human rights violations in the name of public health and collective interest, and there are signs that global democracy has regressed.
However, in Taiwan, we can control the epidemic without sealing off cities and defeat false information without taking them down. "
While the second day of the "democracy summit" was going on, the US Treasury Department announced that it would impose sanctions on 15 individuals and 10 entities in China, North Korea and other countries that were found to have seriously violated human rights, and imposed investment restrictions on a Chinese company that was found to have used surveillance technology to assist in the violation of human rights.
