People's Daily Online - The Gulf Cooperation Council should respond to common challenges
The stopovers of Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman include Oman, United Arab Emirates (UAE), Qatar, Bahrain and Kuwait.
It is worth noting that this is the first visit of Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman to Qatar since Saudi Arabia and its Arab allies imposed a blockade on Qatar in mid-2017, leading to a diplomatic crisis in the Gulf.
In the crisis, Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Bahrain and Egypt have severed diplomatic ties with Qatar and imposed a comprehensive embargo on it, accusing it of supporting terrorist organizations and having close ties with Iran.
The diplomatic tensions have seriously affected the internal unity of the Gulf Cooperation Council and have had a negative impact on the economies of the countries in the region.
Four Arab countries have blocked land, sea and air routes to Qatar.
The crisis was resolved only at the January 2021 Gulf Cooperation Council summit, where the Gulf states signed an agreement to move towards "unity and stability" mediated by the United States and Kuwait.
Saudi Arabia has reopened its border with Qatar.
Saudi Arabia and Egypt are leading the effort to restore relations with Qatar, with the appointment of new ambassadors to the country.
The Gulf Arab countries resolve their differences to restore relations with Qatar, which is based on the requirement of uniting for the interests of all countries and regional stability, and aims to address common challenges.
During the visit of the Saudi Arabian Crown Prince to Oman, the two sides agreed to open the first direct land route between the two Gulf neighbors.
The 725-kilometer-long corridor connecting the two countries will provide convenience for the travel of citizens and integration of supply chain.
The Public Investment Fund (PIF) of Saudi Arabia will invest $5 billion in Oman, and the two countries’ enterprises signed 13 memorandums of understanding worth $30 billion.
The visit of the Saudi Crown Prince to the Gulf countries coincided with the negotiations between Iran and major world powers to save the Iran nuclear agreement, known as the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action (JCPOA), which the Arab Gulf countries condemned because they believed it could not resolve the missile issue with Iran.
The Gulf countries have always regarded Iran as a long-term "competitor" and regarded Iran's nuclear program as a "threat" to regional security.
The Gulf states believe that it is incomplete to not resolve the Iranian missile issue and not take action to curb Iran's activities in the region.
Against the backdrop that the major powers and Iran failed to meet the wishes of the Gulf countries in nuclear negotiations, Saudi Arabia and Iran have conducted direct negotiations.
Its goal is to contain the escalation of tensions in the region.
This is one of the factors that slow down the economic growth of the Gulf Cooperation Council countries.
The upcoming summit of the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) will be the first summit of the GCC countries since the end of the dispute between some member states and Qatar.
This is an opportunity for the Gulf Cooperation Council to re-evaluate its diplomatic achievements in promoting the restoration of the unity of the Gulf Cooperation Council, and at the same time, it will propose various measures to fully cooperate with its member states to meet common challenges and contribute to the stability and development of Gulf countries.
