Asian-American groups complain of Yale and other three Ivy League universities for discrimination in admissions - Xinhuanet
Xinhua News Agency, Washington, May 23 (Reporter Xu Jianmei) More than 130 Asian-American groups filed a complaint with the U.S. Department of Education and the Justice Department on May 23, protesting against discrimination against Asian-American applicants in the enrollment process by three Ivy League universities - Yale University, Brown University and Dartmouth College.
The Asian Education Coalition, which led the appeal, held a press conference in Washington on the same day.
Zhao Yukong, the chairman of the group, said at the press conference that over the past 10 years, the number of Asian applicants who met the admission requirements of American universities has doubled, but the proportion of Asian students in these three American universities has remained at the same level.
Many studies have shown that these universities have "long-term continuity and systematic discrimination against Asian applicants" in the so-called "comprehensive consideration" of the admission process.
Zhao Yunkong said that in 2014, Sanders and other scholars’ research showed that among these three universities, Asian students had the lowest acceptance rate compared to the number of applicants in all ethnic groups.
The data of the U.S. Department of Education also shows that among all Ivy League schools, Brown University and Dartmouth College have the lowest proportion of Asian students admitted, and Yale University and Brown University have implemented de facto racial quotas for Asian applicants for more than 20 years.
Zhao Yukong told Xinhua News Agency that they expect the U.S. Department of Education and Justice to launch an investigation after submitting the appeal, and take measures to prevent these universities from continuing to discriminate against Asians in enrollment after finding out the facts.
They also expect the universities to take the initiative to pay attention to this issue and change their admission policies for Asian students.
At present, the three universities that were complained about have not yet responded.
The Asian American Coalition for Education was founded in 2015, and at that time, it complained about Harvard University's admission discrimination, but the US Department of Education did not accept it for procedural reasons.
In addition to the Chinese-American groups, the Indian-American, Korean-American, Japanese-American and Pakistani-American education groups also participated in this appeal.
