Sabah 3,200 newborns without birth certificates due to shortage of special anti-counterfeiting paper
(Kota Kinabalu, 11th) Sabah National Registration Department said that since the supply of special anti-counterfeiting paper was exhausted, it has been unable to issue birth certificates to newborns since early October, and the number of affected people has reached 3,200.
However, the director of the Bureau, Kalu Amunus, assured that all babies who have applied for birth certificates have been recorded in the system and will be issued when the anti-counterfeiting paper arrives next week.
He made an explanation at the press conference against a web media's report that the printing problem of Sabah Birth Certificate had lasted for 10 months.
He said that the problem had appeared two months ago and Sarawak also faced the same problem.
"One of the reasons is that the Chinese manufacturers of special anti-counterfeiting paper were affected by the epidemic, resulting in the delay of delivery to our country. "
He explained that the anti-counterfeiting paper used in Sabah's birth certificate was different from other regions, so it could not be borrowed from other states.
Kaluaminus revealed that the latest batch of anti-counterfeiting paper had arrived in Kuala Lumpur on the 6th of this month, and was handed over to the printing company on the 8th for partial printing.
"The Bureau will send people to Kuala Lumpur to pick up the goods next Thursday, and will immediately return to Sabah to carry out the final stage of printing. It is estimated that it will take another two working days (December 20) to be delivered to the applicants. "
"We will notify the applicants to go to the local branches to pick up the birth certificates. "
He added that Sabah has issued a total of 24,801 birth certificates so far this year.
