Don't scare people at the starting line - Xinhuanet
When visiting the book market, you can often feel the popularity of self-help books.
Many of today's success books promote the "best principle" - that is, first assume or imply that you can "do", and then tell you what you need to do.
This is equivalent to skipping the most important and fundamental link, that is, "how to do it".
That is why these books have a common feature - either you do your best in one go, or you do nothing at all; either it is 100, or it is 0; either you climb Mount Tai, or you just stay where you are.
This "best practice" is so perfect that it would be recognized as "perfect" by anyone, but 99% of people will be scared to death at the starting line.
Because this theory advocates a path of "you have to make up your mind, change yourself, and make a 180-degree turn", which is exactly the fatal weakness of many people.
For those who stick to old ways, refuse to change, you have to shout in his ear "you should change", which is undoubtedly self-deception and a waste of time.
Most people would be afraid of the word "change", and would be hesitant to make a decision; or they would make a decision to "change" on a whim, and bravely take the first step, but often the good times wouldn't last long, and they would fail after a while, and it would be hard to see sustainability.
Mr. Ma Sanli’s famous cross-talk “Starting from Tomorrow” is a classic that vividly demonstrates this “universal human nature”.
The biggest problem with the "best principle" is that the threshold is too high, which often goes beyond the ability limit of ordinary people, making most people "sigh at the door".
Perhaps, the "second best principle" is a way that may lead more ordinary people to a relatively successful path.
The core of the "second best principle" is to "lower the threshold" - it encourages a theory of "accumulating small victories into big victories", that is, if you can't do 100, just do 1: because 1 is always greater than 0; if you can't climb Mount Tai, just climb the small sand dune in front of your house, because that's better than standing still.
In short, if you do something, it's better than doing nothing.
Due to the significant reduction of threshold, it is more likely for ordinary people to persist and success probability will increase as long as they persist.
It's just that the road to success following the "second best" principle is a little longer.
In short, although the "best principle" is ostensibly designed to make everyone successful, it can ultimately motivate only a small number of people; while the "second best principle" can effectively motivate more people and benefit as many people as possible.
