I just read an article about menstruation, it was so long.
Let me explain to you, men who don't know.
Most girls will experience their first period somewhere between 13 and 15 years old, and will stop menstruating around age 50.
After that, under the premise of good health, it will come once a month in a regular manner.
But it is not regular enough to be predicted accurately, with a tolerance of about one week. You need to prepare in advance, and sometimes you may fail (suddenly come a few days early) if you are unlucky.
The length of time, the amount of blood, and the severity of cramps vary from person to person. Some people have it for three days, some have it for a week; some have little flow, some have heavy bleeding; some have no reaction, some are so painful that they need to be taken to the hospital. The menstrual cramps will change over time. The fact that you did not have menstrual cramps before does not mean that you will not have them in the future.
Menstrual periods are uncontrollable, bleeding 24 hours a day for days at a time from start to finish, and all you can do is use sanitary napkins or tampons to absorb the blood so it doesn't drip on your clothes and chairs.
Dysmenorrhea is mainly caused by the secretion of prostaglandins. If you take ibuprofen at the beginning of the menstrual cycle, it can effectively relieve the pain of the month. It is useless to take ibuprofen after the onset of dysmenorrhea.
Due to uterine congestion and excessive release of prostaglandin, diarrhea is easy to occur before menstruation.
If you have a period and suddenly get diarrhea, you will probably have your period within two days.
It is best to change sanitary napkins every two or three hours, otherwise bacteria will be easily bred. Excluding sleeping time, in the worst case, it should be changed 6 times a day, 7 days a month, which means 42 pieces. If you add the prepared ones in advance, there may be 50 pieces, and it is not one piece per month.
(This is modified here. Tampons do not need to be changed frequently. You can change it every five or six hours, and at most every eight hours.)
Menstrual hormones can make you more irritable, and ovulation can also cause mood swings due to hormonal changes in your body, so it's just bad luck, your body is making things difficult for itself.
But official crime figures suggest that women's ability to control their emotions is still high and they are not using emotional issues as an excuse.
It is not just blood that flows, but also the detached endometrium.
The endometrium is the thinnest after menstruation, and then gradually thickens. It can be understood that the uterus is a house, and every month builds a house (the endometrium thickens) to welcome guests. If the guest does not come, it will be demolished (menstruation discharged), and a new house will be built next month, and so on.
Menstruation is inconvenient. For decades, you bleed for several days every month for 24 hours and have abdominal pain and backache. Who would like to?
But the reason you were born is because your mother had a period, and the period is to prepare the house for you to live in for 10 months when you first come to this world. Everyone should thank their mother for having a regular and healthy period.
In the past, many things of women were suppressed, and society brainwashed women to say that it was dirty and shameful. Everyone thought it was like that, but times have changed.
