Metaphysics is the study of questions that cannot be answered directly through perception, but through rational reasoning and logic.
Metaphysics is the fundamental principle of philosophy and aims to explain the basic nature of existence and the world.
The Chinese translation “metaphysics” is taken from the sentence “the metaphysical is called the way, and the physical is called the vessel” in “Yi Jing • Xi Ci Shang Chuan”. It was translated by the Japanese Tetsusaburo Inoue (Meiji era) from the word “metaphysic”.
Metaphysical branches include ontology, natural theology and its extended problems.
Ontology is "the study of which nouns represent real entities and which nouns represent a concept".
Metaphysical naturalism is the denial of the existence of God.
Its theoretical basis is that all phenomena can be explained by the concept of natural reason, and it was proposed by philosophers who supported science and evolution.
Methodological naturalism, on the other hand, refers to a method of research that ignores God.
Spiritual philosophy is a branch of philosophy that studies the nature of consciousness and its relationship to the physical body, especially the brain.
Dualism and monism are the two main schools of thought in the discussion of "the relationship between mind and body".
Epistemology is a branch of philosophy that investigates the nature, origins, and scope of knowledge.
Ethics is the discipline of systematic thought and study of the moral life of human beings; here, morality is defined as all the codes of conduct that are accepted by a group of people or a culture.
It is divided into meta-ethics (the abstract study of the nature of ethical theories or critiques) and normative ethics (the evaluation of different moral views and the giving of moral prescriptions about what is right or wrong to do.
It studies what kind of moral code people should follow), applied ethics (the academic study of applying ethical theory to real-life situations, including bioethics, political ethics, etc.), descriptive ethics (the collection of information about how people live, from observed patterns).
Its examination of ethics is not based on anticipated theories, but on real choices made by actual moral agents (moral agents) and observed, with all moral factors that contribute to these decisions, it examines the ethics held by social groups).
The history of ethics includes Greek and Roman ethics, and medieval Christian ethics.
Part I: Humanistic ethics, represented by Valla and Montaigne.
Part II: The ethical thought of the Reformation, represented by Calvin.
Part III: Political Ethics, represented by Machiavelli and others, Modern Ethics (17-18th century, British empiricist ethics, representative figures: Hume and others, 17-18th century, Continental rationalism ethics, representative figures: Spinoza and others, 18th century, French Enlightenment ethics, representative figures: Voltaire and others, 18-19th century, German academism ethics, representative figures: Kant and Hegel and others, 18-19th century, British utilitarianism ethics, representative figures: Bentham and Sikivik and others, 19th century, British evolutionary ethics, representative figures: Spencer and Huxley and others, late 19th century, German neo-Kantian ethics, representative figures: Liekier and others, late 19th century, neo-Hegelian ethics, representative figures: Thomas Hill Green and others), Transitional Ethics (irrationalist ethics, representative figures: Kierkegaard and Schopenhauer and Nietzsche and others).
Logic is the study of "the principles and standards of valid reasoning and proof".
Logic usually includes three parts: inductive reasoning, abductive reasoning and deductive reasoning, which can be divided into formal logic and informal logic.
Aesthetics is a discipline that is concerned with the study of the nature and meaning of beauty.
