In the past two years, the blockade and encirclement of Huawei chips by the United States has not only put Huawei in an unprecedented predicament, but also caused huge losses to "American" enterprises.
The United States suppresses China's chip industry and requires its own enterprises and relevant enterprises of its allies to block China's chip industry at the same time, which directly leads to the consequences that the orders and profits of global chip suppliers are affected, resulting in the research and development investment and production capacity of chip production enterprises decreasing, and thus causing the shortage and disorder of the global chip supply chain.
Now, the "chip shortage" has affected all major industries around the world, such as the suspension of production of automobile companies and the price increase of mobile phones. Almost all industries that use chips are deeply affected.
In order to master the "chip" autonomy, more and more technology giants in many countries have begun to take the path of "de-Americanization".
In order to firmly control the global semiconductor control right and restrain the development of China's semiconductor industry, the old Americans have recently taken frequent actions, and they are holding the "Chip Summit"; they are also inviting the giants of 64 countries to establish a semiconductor alliance, and they are allocating funds to support the local technology giants in the United States. For example, the old technology giant Intel in the United States spent $20 billion to build two wafer processing plants in Arizona, United States, in order to regain the leading position in the chip field, and opened the chip OEM business to the outside world.
Most people thought that Intel's move would have a certain impact on TSMC and would likely take away TSMC's "job".
This is because Intel, as a long-established technology giant in the United States, has been deeply involved in the semiconductor industry for decades, has a large number of talent teams and technical reserves, and is sure to rise quickly in the short term and defeat Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company.
However, for Intel's decision, the founder of TSMC, Jerry Yang, did not care.
At the "2021 Master Think Tank Forum" held in April this year, Zhang Zhonglou said something shocking. He said without hesitation that Intel's investment to build a wafer factory was quite ironic.
The reason why Morris Chang is quite disdainful of Intel’s entry into the wafer field is that he still holds a grudge over a grudge between TSMC and Intel more than 30 years ago.
In the 1960s and 1970s, Intel has always been a pearl in the manufacturing industry of the United States, occupying the top position in the world, and is the world's semiconductor field hegemon.
By the 1980s, the world's high-end semiconductor chips have been controlled by a few strong players.
At that time, the semiconductor industry was characterized by the IDM model, and chips were designed, manufactured and packaged by a single factory. Intel was the representative of that era.
However, such a model has put some small businesses under great pressure. Many semiconductor entrepreneurs have been deterred from entering the market due to huge production line construction costs.
In the 1980s, inspired by market demand, the chip industry began to see a phenomenon of division of labor and cooperation. In 1984, when Morris Chang heard that there was a company specializing in chip design, he thought that since there was a company specializing in design, then a factory dedicated to wafer manufacturing could be established.
Soon after, Zhang Chongmei’s vision became a reality, and TSMC was born.
TSMC has focused its main business on the single link of “wafer processing”. It does not design, but only provides wafer manufacturing services.
At that time, the newly established Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company was just a small player in the face of the giant Intel.
In order to obtain capital support, Zhang Chongmou once sought investment from Intel, but at that time, Intel, after hearing that TSMC only did the manufacturing link of the chip industry, was obviously very arrogant and looked down on TSMC, and resolutely refused to invest in TSMC.
Zhang Chongmei also believed that Intel did not respect the wafer process service at that time.
It is not surprising that the giant who looked down on wafer manufacturing services would also start to do wafer fabrication.
So, Zhang Chongmei thought that Intel’s entry into wafer foundry was very ironic.
Now, TSMC’s market value has surpassed that of Intel.
Faced with the strong attack of Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company, Intel naturally dared not to be complacent.
Now, TSMC has rushed towards 3nm chips, while Intel is still stuck at 14nm seven years ago.
It is not easy to catch up.
No more waiting.
