Starry Sky Appointment | The Geminid Meteor Shower Will Reach Its Maximum on the 14th - Xinhuanet
Xinhua News Agency, Tianjin, December 12 (Reporter Zhou Rujian) According to astronomical science popularization experts, as the last large-scale meteor shower of the year, the Geminid meteor shower, one of the three major meteor showers in the Northern Hemisphere, will reach its maximum on December 14. Friends who like meteor showers can observe it in the latter half of the night.
A Beijing astronomy enthusiast An Jiu took this photo of the Geminid meteor shower in Qinghai on December 13, 2018.
Most meteor showers are caused by periodic comets, but the parent body of the Geminid meteor shower is the asteroid 3200 Phaethon, an Apollo asteroid.
The Geminid meteor shower is also the second known meteor shower that is not "born" from a comet.
Xiu Lipeng, a council member of Tianjin Astronomical Society and an astronomy popularization expert, said that just like the New Year’s blockbuster, the Geminid meteor shower generally appears at the end of the year, and its “release period” can last from December 4 to 17.
This meteor shower is known for its large and stable flow, and together with Quadrantid meteor shower and Perseid meteor shower, it is called the three major meteor showers in the Northern Hemisphere.
A notable feature of the Geminid meteor shower is that the meteoroids move slowly, are colorful, and there are many fireballs. It is suitable for both visual observation and astrophotography for beginners.
A Geminid meteor shower photographed by Yang Jing, an astronomy enthusiast in Tianjin, on December 13, 2020, in Jizhou, Tianjin.
According to the astronomical forecast, the Geminid meteor shower this year will reach its peak at about 15:00 on December 14, and the ZHR (the number of meteors that can be seen per hour when the radiant point is directly above the head under ideal observation conditions) will be 150.
The recommended index map of Geminid meteor shower.
"Last year, the 'dazzling autumn' Geminid meteor shower left a deep impression on people, but this year's Geminid meteor shower will be affected by moonlight, and the brilliance will be slightly discounted.
The 14th is November 11 of the lunar calendar, and the phase of the moon is a full gibbous moon. The bright moonlight will interfere with observations. Fortunately, the moon will set in the second half of the night, and at this time, the radiant point of the Geminid meteor shower will also rise relatively high.
Friends who like meteor showers can observe them in the last few hours after the moon sets in the second half of the night, but they should pay attention to keeping warm. "
How can we see as many meteors as possible?
"It is not recommended to observe in the city, you can choose a high altitude, low light pollution and open view in the northeast direction in the suburbs or countryside, and then wait patiently until the moon falls, and then observe with the naked eye.
It should be noted that the Geminid meteor shower has a large flow in all directions, so there is no need to keep staring at the radiant point of the Geminid meteor shower during observation.
For those who like photography, if you want to take a brilliant "meteor shower film", it is best to choose a wide-angle, large aperture lens, and use high ISO and maximum aperture when shooting.
Based on stability and continuity considerations, tripods, spare batteries and shutter release cables are also essential. "
Xiu Lipeng reminded.
What is the difference between laser TV and traditional TV in terms of market positioning?
In my understanding, the traditional television referred to by the main question should be the liquid crystal TV. The market positioning differences between laser TV and liquid crystal TV mainly include the following two points:
First, laser TVs are more focused on the large screen market.
Compared with the market positioning of LCD TVs, laser TVs are obviously more focused on the large screen market, or rather, laser TVs are products that are born for large screens.
At present, the mainstream LCD TV size is generally between 55 inches and 65 inches, while the size of laser TV is generally above 75 inches, and the largest is more than 100 inches, which fully meets the user's demand for large screen TVs.
In recent years, the shipment and sales volume of laser TVs have been increasing worldwide, accounting for more than 60% of the overall TV sales volume of 80 inches and above.
According to the prediction of CIC, in 2020, the sales volume of laser TV market will increase by more than 140% and continue to maintain high growth.
The reason for such good performance in the large screen market is mainly due to the excellent performance of laser TVs in terms of eye protection.
Based on the imaging principle of diffuse reflection of laser TV, the light emitted by the laser light source does not directly enter the eyeball. Compared with the backlight source of LCD TV, the imaging process is closer to the imaging process of objects under natural conditions, and the stimulation to the eyes is also smaller.
The China Electronics Standardization Institute’s Sai Xi Laboratory and Beijing Union Medical College Hospital’s ophthalmology experts obtained 144 sets of test data through strict comparative experiments under the same conditions for watching laser TVs and LCD TVs.
The data confirms that laser TVs do have strong eye protection characteristics, and even have a certain improvement effect in specific aspects.
In the current situation where visual health is receiving increasing attention, laser TVs have not only attracted more and more users with their eye protection advantages, but also opened another breakthrough in the color TV market.
Second, laser TVs are more focused on the high-end market.
In contrast to the continuous decline in the price of LCD TVs, the current market price of laser TVs is mostly above 10,000 yuan, and the price gradually climbs with the upgrade of size and light source.
Why is the laser TV so expensive?
It's definitely a valid point.
With the continuous development of light sources and technology levels, laser TV has many advantages that liquid crystal TVs and projectors cannot achieve.
With ultra-short focal length projection technology, the projection distance of laser TV is shorter than that of traditional projection equipment, which can achieve a 100-inch large screen experience in a short distance, requires little space, and the main unit is small and occupies very little space.
In terms of brightness, big brand laser TVs can reach more than 2000 lumens.
In addition, the heat is lower, so the equipment loss is small, and the service life of the TV is longer.
In addition, many high-end laser TV models also have true 4K ultra-high definition picture quality, artificial intelligence systems, and bionic screens, among many other cutting-edge technologies, which can bring users more shocking audio-visual experience.
It is not a pseudo-demand, but a product of demand segmentation. In terms of picture quality and input-output ratio, laser TV is not suitable for audio-visual players. If you want to balance the large screen and insufficient viewing environment, you can try it. There are pros and cons to everything. After weighing the pros and cons, you can make your own judgment. Just be afraid of believing one-sided words.
In short, the ultra-wide-angle near-focus projector - laser TV, in order to get close to the screen, it has to use expensive ultra-wide-angle lenses and reflectors, which is not in line with the best optical design.
In order to block some ambient light, the use of micro-structured Fresnel curtains is costly, and the price of 80 inches can basically be exchanged for a 65-inch LCD.
And one more thing, I really hate it when projection manufacturers use the fact that diffuse reflection is good for the eyes as a selling point. Please tell me, the light on the screen that the eye can see is direct light to the eye. You diffuse it onto the ceiling, but the eye cannot see it. So you insist that LCD TVs are also "direct" light? The ceiling will be illuminated when you turn on the TV, right?
Moreover, if the projection screen is not diffused reflection, it cannot form an image.
The hot spot (light spot) effect of high gain screen is ignored, and a lot of light is reflected to the eyes at a certain angle, which is really uncomfortable.
